{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable (low) outcomes compared to favorable (high) outcomes. In this case, we assess the skewness of both lotteries.\n\nLottery A: Offers 2400 Euros with 100% probability. This is a deterministic outcome with no variability; thus, it has zero skewness (symmetric).\n\nLottery B: Offers 2500 Euros with 33% probability, 2400 Euros with 66% probability, and 0 Euros with 1% probability. The outcome of 0 Euros (a loss relative to 2400) occurs with only 1% probability, while the favorable outcomes (2400 and 2500) dominate. The probability mass is concentrated on higher values (66% + 33% = 100%), with only a small 1% chance of a very unfavorable outcome. Therefore, Lottery B is not right-skewed; in fact, it is left-skewed or nearly symmetric, because the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) is rare and the favorable outcomes (2400 and 2500) dominate. A right-skewed lottery would have a tail of low outcomes with high probability. Here, the low outcome (0) has only 1% probability, so the distribution is actually left-skewed (a long tail on the low side). Thus, the decision reason, which prefers the more right-skewed lottery, does not apply to this decision problem because neither lottery is right-skewed — in fact, Lottery B is left-skewed. Therefore, the decision reason is not applicable in this context.",
  "step_2": "The individual reflects on Lottery A, describing it as having a high, certain payoff of 2400 Euros. For Lottery B, they note that while there is only a 1% chance of getting 0 Euros (a loss), there is a 66% chance of getting 2400 Euros and a 33% chance of getting 2500 Euros. The individual acknowledges that the maximum gain in Lottery B (2500 Euros) is only 100 Euros more than Lottery A, and despite the small 1% risk of losing all money, they find the risk not worth it. The justification centers on the magnitude of the potential gain relative to the risk, and the individual concludes that the small risk is not justified by the small gain. There is no mention of skewness, distribution tails, or any evaluation of whether one lottery is more right-skewed than the other. The deliberation focuses on risk versus reward, specifically the small potential gain versus a small risk of loss, not on the statistical skewness of the outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not present in the individual’s consideration. The individual does not evaluate the distribution of outcomes in terms of tail probabilities or skewness. In fact, Lottery B has a very small probability (1%) of a very unfavorable outcome (0 Euros), which makes it left-skewed, not right-skewed. The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which would have a larger probability of low outcomes. Since neither lottery is right-skewed, and the individual does not reference skewness at all, the reason was not used. The individual’s justification is based on risk-reward trade-off, not on skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not applied in the deliberation process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
