  "step_2": "The individual states that in both lotteries, it is most likely to get neither a win nor a loss (i.e., 0 Euros), which reflects the high probability of the unfavorable outcome. The individual notes that the chance of winning differs only by 1000 Euros (2500 vs. 2400), and that the probability of winning is slightly higher in Lottery B (34% vs. 33%). The individual concludes that the lotteries are 'in etwa gleich gut' (about equally good) and that the decision was made spontaneously. There is no mention of skewness, the distribution of probabilities across outcomes, or any comparison based on the relative weight of unfavorable outcomes. The justification centers on the similarity of outcomes and the small differences in winning probabilities, not on the skewness of the lotteries or the dominance of the 0-Euro outcome.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — specifically, the higher probability of 0 Euros in Lottery A (67% vs. 66%) indicating greater right-skewness — is acknowledged in the verbal report in the sense that both lotteries have a high chance of yielding 0 Euros. However, the individual does not explicitly or implicitly evaluate the skewness of the lotteries or compare the extent to which the unfavorable outcome dominates. Instead, the individual focuses on the small difference in winning amounts and probabilities, concluding the lotteries are similar. The justification does not reflect the rule of preferring the more right-skewed lottery. Therefore, the decision reason was not used in the deliberation process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
